{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "thm:relative-eventual-certification",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Fix the substantive finite design data in \\(\\Xi\\), including \\(M,\\iota,\\mathcal T,\\mathfrak G,(\\Theta_{\\boldsymbol d},\\Theta^{\\mathrm{in}}_{\\boldsymbol d}),q,W,\\rho,r_{\\mathrm{id}},\\alpha,w_{\\max}\\), and \\(N_{\\max}\\), and consider a sequence \\((\\Xi_t)\\) that changes only the truncation, Taylor, subdivision, and outward-rounding controls.  Define the exact worst-case contrast variance and its exact well requirement by\n\\[\n v_\\tau^\\circ=\\max_{\\boldsymbol d\\in\\mathfrak G}\n \\max_{\\theta\\in\\Theta^{\\mathrm{in}}_{\\boldsymbol d}}\n \\max_{1\\leq a\\leq2K}\n [V_\\tau(\\boldsymbol d,\\theta;\\iota)]_{aa},\n \\qquad\n N_{\\mathrm{req}}^\\circ=\n z_{1-\\alpha/2}^2v_\\tau^\\circ/w_{\\max}^2.\n\\]\nSuppose the exact syntax and box tests pass, \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)=K\\), every \\(\\rho_j>0\\), and there are strict positive uniform exact margins for every nonempty wrong-graph comparison, every nonempty remote within-graph comparison, every required local chart strong-monotonicity and Jacobian test, every required interiority, denominator, and positive-definiteness test, and the contrast-precision inequality \\(N_{\\mathrm{req}}^\\circ<N_{\\max}\\).  If the Poisson-tail errors, Taylor-enclosure widths, terminal objective and chart mesh diameters, and outward-rounding errors all converge uniformly to zero, and the deterministic chart trees eventually give a valid cover with the stated local margin, then \\(\\mathfrak C(\\Xi_t)=\\mathtt{CERTIFIED}\\) for all sufficiently large \\(t\\).  This is a relative eventual-certification result; it does not assert that either prescribed synthetic benchmark satisfies its strict-slack or convergence hypotheses.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "certificate-input",
      "certificate-output",
      "certificate-status",
      "composition",
      "dimension-types",
      "moment-grid",
      "candidate-graph-tuples",
      "graph-parameter-space",
      "inference-parameter-space",
      "exponent-map",
      "weight",
      "allocation",
      "injectivity-radius",
      "confidence-level",
      "confidence-halfwidth",
      "precision-budget",
      "required-wells",
      "chart-projection-cover",
      "effect-covariance",
      "designated-coordinate-embedding"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:certificate-input",
      "def:certificate-procedure",
      "def:grid-criterion",
      "def:replicate-pgf-covariance",
      "def:sandwich-covariance",
      "lem:polynomial-pgf-regularity",
      "thm:certificate-soundness-complexity"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(g_*>0\\), \\(\\lambda_*>0\\), \\(\\kappa_*>0\\), \\(\\mu_*>0\\), \\(b_*>0\\), and \\(s_*>0\\) be lower bounds for the strict exact margins in the statement, with the graph or remote constant omitted when its comparison set is empty.  The parameter boxes, their finite products, the finite graph set, and the fixed grid are compact.  All exact retained targets are continuous by \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}polynomial\\text{-}pgf\\text{-}regularity}\\).\n\nFor a wrong-graph product, its exact objective \\(f\\) satisfies \\(f\\geq g_*\\) on the whole compact product.  Uniform convergence of the tail-adjusted Taylor and rounding enclosures therefore makes every terminal lower enclosure exceed \\(g_*/2\\) for all sufficiently large \\(t\\).\n\nThe remote branch requires a separate boundary argument.  On one same-graph product define\n\\[\n D(\\theta,\\theta')=\\|\\theta-\\theta'\\|_2^2-r_{\\mathrm{id}}^2,\n \\qquad\n f(\\theta,\\theta')=\n \\|m(\\theta)-m(\\theta')\\|_W^2,\n\\]\nand \\(S=\\{D\\geq0\\}\\).  By hypothesis, \\(f\\geq\\lambda_*>0\\) on the compact set \\(S\\).  Thus\n\\(U=\\{f>\\lambda_*/2\\}\\) is an open neighborhood of \\(S\\).  The two open sets \\(U\\) and \\(\\{D<0\\}\\) cover the whole compact product.  Compactness gives a positive scale \\(a\\) such that every product box of diameter below \\(a\\) lies in at least one member of this cover: otherwise there would be boxes of diameter tending to zero, points in them, and a convergent subsequence whose limit belonged to neither open set.  For large \\(t\\), the terminal mesh is below \\(a\\).  Uniform enclosure convergence then makes the lower interval for \\(f\\) positive on every leaf contained in \\(U\\), while the upper interval for \\(D\\) is negative on every leaf contained in \\(\\{D<0\\}\\) after any necessary further subdivisions.  Crucially, a leaf contained in \\(U\\) passes from positivity of \\(f\\) even when it straddles \\(D=0\\).  This is exactly the corrected remote-subset rule; no positive lower bound on \\(D\\) is required.\n\nThe finitely many exact symmetric chart matrices have eigenvalues at least \\(\\mu_*\\), and the exact matrices \\(J^{\\mathsf T}J\\) have eigenvalues at least \\(\\kappa_*^2\\).  Uniform derivative-enclosure and rounding convergence makes the corresponding interval \\(LDL^{\\mathsf T}\\) tests succeed at some positive dyadic values below these margins.  By hypothesis the deterministic inflated charts eventually pass their finite coverage check.  The interiority and required algebraic denominator or pivot tests similarly succeed because their exact margins are at least \\(b_*\\) or the stipulated positive denominator margin.\n\nFinally, let \\(N_{\\max}-N_{\\mathrm{req}}^\\circ\\geq s_*>0\\).  Tail, Taylor, mesh, and rounding convergence makes the outward variance upper bound, and hence its continuous required-well transform, differ from \\(N_{\\mathrm{req}}^\\circ\\) by less than \\(s_*/2\\) for all sufficiently large \\(t\\).  The certified required-well bound is then below \\(N_{\\max}\\).  There are only finitely many graph, remote, chart, covariance, and contrast tasks, so one index works simultaneously for all of them.  The unconditional termination and one-sided soundness proved in \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}certificate\\text{-}soundness\\text{-}complexity}\\), together with the literal return rule in \\(\\mathrm{def{:}certificate\\text{-}procedure}\\), now forces \\(\\mathfrak C(\\Xi_t)=\\mathtt{CERTIFIED}\\) for every sufficiently large \\(t\\).  None of these hypotheses has been verified for \\(\\Xi_0\\) or \\(\\Xi_1\\), so no benchmark conclusion follows.",
    "justification": "This is the precise relative completeness counterpart to the unconditional finite-termination and one-sided-soundness theorem.",
    "gap": "Finite interval procedures need not certify at fixed controls; eventual success follows only from strict exact slack and uniform convergence of every enclosure and mesh used by the return rule.",
    "consumer": "It explains when increasing declared numerical controls will eventually turn a genuinely separated prospective design into a certified one, without asserting that either synthetic benchmark qualifies.",
